.TH PUT7 1C UniSoft
.SH NAME
put7 \- puts a file onto a remote machine.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B put7
[
.B \-p
.I port
] [
.B \-sSPEED
] [
.B \-i[ID]
]
.I fromfile
[
.I tofile
]
.br
.B put7
[
.B \-p
.I port
] [
.B \-sSPEED
]
.B \-c
.I command
[
.I args
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Put7\^
is part of system of programs useful for transferring files between 
.SM UNIX
systems.  It is the "uploader" designed to transmit files from a local
machine to a remote machine.  For a brief discussion of the take/put
system and installation instructions, see the companion document:
"\fIOverview of the UniSoft Take/Put File Transfer System\fR".
.PP
The default
port is 
.BR /dev/tty0 ;
the 
.B \-p
option can be used to specify an alternate output port.
The default speed is determined by the system; the
.B \-s
option can be used to specify a speed.
If
.I tofile\^
is unspecified, then it is assumed to be the same as
.IR fromfile .
If
.I fromfile\^
is a directory,
.I tofile\^
must be a directory on the remote machine (or if nonexistent, the
last existent directory specified in the pathname must be writable).
.PP
The
.BR \-i [ \s-1ID\s-1 ]
option specifies a system 
.SM ID
and is the
mechanism for remapping pathnames on the remote machine.
The system 
.SM ID
is passed to the remote machine where
it is used to generate pathname prefixes (using the
.B /etc/take_oem
file) which are appended to the
.I tofile\^
pathname supplied by
.IR put7 .
If an 
.SM ID 
is specified when using the 
.B \-i 
option, it is used on the
remote machine. 
If no 
.SM ID
is specified, the default 
.SM ID
is 
read from the
.B /etc/sys_id
file if it exists; if the 
.B /etc/sys_id
does not exist, the system 
.SM ID
is considered to be the user name of
the invoker of
.I put6\^
(i.e., the user who logged in over the port used).
.PP
The
.B \-c
option is useful for executing an arbitrary command on the remote machine.
All arguments following the
.B \-c
flag are collected, transmitted to the remote machine and executed as a single
command.  The standard input to the
.I put7\^
program is sent to the remote machine to become the standard input to the
command specified.  The standard error of the remote command becomes the
standard error of
.IR put7 .
The standard output of the remote command is not returned.
The exit status of the remote command is returned as the exit status of
.IR put7 .
.PP
In order to perform its function,
.IR put7\^ (1C)
interfaces with the program 
.B /usr/bin/put6 
on the remote machine.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
put7 /a/b/c
.PP
puts the contents of the directory (or file) "/a/b/c" on the local machine
into a similarly named directory (or file) on the
remote machine; if "/a/b/c" did not previously exist on the remote
machine, it is created; otherwise it is overwritten.
.IP
put7 file.c /x/y/z
.PP
puts the contents of "file.c" on the local machine 
into "/x/y/z/file.c" on the remote machine.  Note that
"file.c" is created on the remote machine if "z" is a 
directory; if "z" is a file rather than a directory, its 
contents are overwritten but its name remains "z" rather
than becoming "file.c".
.SH FILES
.TP "\w'\fBfromfile\fR\ \ \ \ 'u"
fromfile
The local file name.  When using the
.B \-i
option, this file should be specified as a pathname 
starting at the root of the local machine.
.ns
.TP
file
The remote file name; if 
.I tofile\^
is null, 
.I tofile\^ 
is defaulted to 
.IR fromfile .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cu(1C),
take7(1)
.br
.I "Overview of the UniSoft Take/Put File Transfer System"
.\"	@(#)put7.1	5.1 of 11/16/83
